# Firmware build artifacts.
#
# Allowlist, not blocklist. This was "kitsune-custom-*" and a padded image built
# for one experiment was named differently, so it sailed straight past and sat
# there untracked-but-visible. Ignore the whole output directory and name the two
# files that are meant to be committed.
#
# It matters twice over: a customised build carries YOUR hostnames, and every one
# of these is Hello's firmware code (see NOTICE).
sense/firmware/kitsune-4513/out/*
!sense/firmware/kitsune-4513/out/kitsune.bin
sense/firmware/kitsune-voice/out/*
!sense/firmware/kitsune-voice/out/kitsune.bin

# out/kitsune.out is NOT committed, on purpose. The .bin is byte-reproducible;
# the ELF it comes from is not. armcl stamps ten random six-character suffixes
# into .strtab on every run (e.g. ".debug_frame\0 05538 WLXv3p"), so a verifying
# rebuild left the repo dirty in 60 bytes that cannot affect anything. None of
# those bytes are in a loadable section, which is exactly why the .bin still
# matches. kitsune_reord.out is the committed reference ELF for elfdiff.py and
# funcmp.py, so nothing is lost.
